  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another frosty morning tomorrow, but slightly\nwarmer than this morning with potential for winds to remain\nlight opposed to dead-calm. MinTs again AoB freezing across the\nCoastal Plain where there is a greater chance of being decoupled\nlonger. Inner Banks, temps near sunrise will be closer to the \nupper 30s to low 40s, with no plans for a frost advisory. Upper\n20s in our usual cold/sheltered spots remain possible, but not\nas widespread as this morning.\n\nLow-level thicknesses increasing supports steady warming mid to\nlate week, and even though high pressure will remain in place, \ntemps will warm to above climo with 70s inland. For the OBX, \nhighs will be tempered by light northeast breezes off the cooler\nwaters of the Labrador Current as water temps have cooled to \nthe upper 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure over the Ohio Valley will dominate\nour weather all week, settling overhead by the end of the week,\nand off the coast by Saturday. As a result dry conditions will\npersist with plenty of sunshine. Fire weather concerns may grow\nwith the curing vegetation combined with low RH values each \nafternoon. Luckily winds will not be an issue with a light \ngradient in place. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...The potential for a coastal low to develop and \ngain strength off the FL coast late this week in response to a \nshortwave digging across the Gulf above SFC coastal troughing \nongoing along the FL leg of the Gulfstream this afternoon \ncontinues to grow. However, what happens once a pressure center \npresents itself remains fairly uncertain with the typical \nquestions for a day 5+ low off the coast: where the system will\ntrack, how much it deepens, how fast is it moving, etc... \nInitially a generally Nward motion is expected. Two scenarios\nthat would lead to more impactful solutions are one, a faster \nbut weaker low approaching the SC coast and then passing over or\neven W of the FA before exiting the MidAtlantic coast overnight\nSUN. This solution would result in more convective rain and\na higher thunderstorm probability. The second progression is a \nslower one, with a deeper low remaining offshore. This would \nlead to stronger winds and greater coastal implications but with\nstorm-total rainfall accumulations an order of magnitude less\nand significantly less tstorm chances than scenario 1. We will\ncontinue to monitor the system, as large waves and associated \ncoastal impacts are becoming more likely along the Outer Banks.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...King Tide Cycle starts second half of this\nweekend, peaking middle of next week. This higher than normal\nastronomical tide cycle is slightly stronger than the one that\noccurred the last week of OCT. Will have to see how the system\nin Key Message 3 progresses before we can determine what sort of\neffect this King Tide cycle will have on our coast.",
